<section id="seasonal-patterns">
  <h2>3. Seasonal & Calendar-Based Patterns</h2>
  <p>We test for statistically significant seasonal effects using one-way ANOVA across months and days of week, supplemented by one-sample t-tests against zero.</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <h3>Monthly Returns</h3>
      <p><strong>ANOVA result:</strong> F = 0.83, <em>p</em> = 0.613 — we <strong>fail to reject</strong> the null hypothesis of equal monthly means. Observed differences are likely noise.</p>
      <p>That said, January (+2.32% avg, <em>p</em> = 0.014) and August (+1.97% avg, <em>p</em> = 0.031) show marginal statistical significance. June is the weakest month (–0.92% avg), though not significant (<em>p</em> = 0.347).</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Day-of-Week Returns</h3>
      <p><strong>ANOVA result:</strong> F = 0.79, <em>p</em> = 0.534 — no significant difference across weekdays.</p>
      <p>Friday (+0.093% avg, <em>p</em> = 0.005) and Monday (+0.065% avg, <em>p</em> = 0.041) are marginally significant, possibly reflecting weekend risk-premium accumulation and rebalancing flows.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

