  <p>The negative skew (–0.32) and high excess kurtosis (+6.53) indicate:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fat tails</strong> — extreme moves occur far more often than a normal distribution predicts.</li>
    <li><strong>Mild left skew</strong> — large negative surprises are slightly more frequent than large positive ones, though the largest single-day move was actually a loss (–11.37% on 2026-01-30).</li>
    <li><strong>Convex crisis behavior</strong> — during stress, gold often rallies (positive tail), but liquidity crunches can generate sharp temporary selloffs.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="highlight-box regime-high">
    <strong>Risk management implication:</strong> A 99% VaR of –3.05% implies that on 1% of trading days, gold loses more than 3% in a single session. For a $100M allocation, that is a &gt;$3M daily mark-to-market swing. Position sizing and options overlays should account for this tail thickness, not a Gaussian assumption.
